 US long-bond yields renew 24-year high on resilient US spending
 The Fed’s preferred measure of inflation, the personal consumption expenditures price index, increased 0.3% from a month earlier
 Bloomberg 
 September 30, 2026 / 21:49 IST 
 --> 
 The Fed’s preferred measure of inflation, the personal consumption expenditures price index, increased 0.3% from a month earlier. 
 Long-dated Treasury yields hit multi-decade highs.
 US consumer spending rose at fastest pace in over a year.
 Treasury plans to accept up to $6 billion in long-term debt.
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
Long-dated Treasury yields rose to fresh multi-decade highs as resilient US spending bolstered expectations that the US economy can handle higher interest rates.
Yields on 30-year Treasuries rose as much as six basis points on Wednesday to 5.63%, the highest since 2002, after US consumer spending rose in August at the fastest pace in over a year and amid heavy corporate supply. The US also announced plans to accept up to $6 billion in long-term debt on Thursday as part of Secretary Scott Bessent’s expanded buyback program.
“It feels like a buyers’ strike, really,” said John Briggs, head of US rates strategy at Natixis Corporate & Investment Banking. “The price action overall remains terrible.”
Thirty-year yields have been hitting milestone after milestone in a bruising global bond selloff driven by inflationary risks, resilient economic data and fiscal concerns. A heavy corporate supply slate also fueled some of the moves this week.
A Bloomberg index of global government debt has fallen 2.1% since June, the biggest move since the three-month stretch at the end of 2024, when Donald Trump won a second term as US president and investors braced for a more expansionary fiscal policy.
“It’s a continuation of bearish momentum,” Dan Carter, senior portfolio manager at Fort Washington Investment Advisors, said. “A lot of investors have already been positioned for lower rates, so may not be a lot of dry powder to add.”
On Wednesday, those declines extended in longer-dated Treasuries after data showed a resilient economy that should enable the Federal Reserve to keep raising rates in the coming months to help control price pressures. Inflation-adjusted personal spending climbed, while ADP Research’s private payroll data increased in September more than economists forecast.
The stronger economic data keeps the curve steeper and “long-end rates a touch higher,” said John Canavan, an analyst at Oxford Economics. He added that “traders may also be hesitant to make any major adjustments” before Friday’s nonfarm payrolls report.
They held their losses as the Treasury unveiled its plans to repurchase securities maturing in 10 to 20 years, the latest in Secretary Scott Bessent’s expanded program meant to help contain yields. Officials had also laid out plans to buy up to $6 billion at the prior two long-term debt operations, but disappointed investors by accepting less.
“I think the buybacks are having the biggest impact on liquidity conditions and spreads,” said Angelo Manolatos, rates strategist at Wells Fargo Securities. “A negative growth catalyst will likely be needed to spark a sustained move lower in the longer-term rates.”
At the other end of the US maturity spectrum, two-year yields, most sensitive to the Fed’s policy path, declined by as much as five basis points to 4.82%, the lowest in just over a week, after a softer-than-expected reading of the Fed’s preferred inflation gauge. They later pared the move.
The data led traders to pare back their expectations for the Fed to raise interest rates in October, pricing in about a 36% chance of a move, compared to roughly coin-toss odds seen prior to the data.
“This helped markets breathe sigh of relief given the focus on inflation,” said Gennadiy Goldberg, head of US interest-rates strategy at TD Securities. “But markets remain hesitant to rally too much given the uncertainty about methodology changes.”
The Fed’s preferred measure of inflation, the personal consumption expenditures price index, increased 0.3% from a month earlier. Excluding food and energy, the PCE price index rose 0.2%, lower than the median forecast of 0.3% by economists surveyed by Bloomberg. The release included some methodological changes in the way inflation is calculated.
Expectations for an October rate increase had been slipping since Tuesday, when New York Fed’s John Williams said “one further upward adjustment” to the central bank’s target range for a US overnight lending rate “may be appropriate late this year” to contain inflation. Fed officials lifted their benchmark rate for the first time since 2023 earlier this month.
Later on Wednesday, Treasuries may benefit from anticipation of buying tied to the monthly rebalancing of bond indexes at 4 p.m. New York time. The rebalancing, in which qualifying bonds sold during the month are added to indexes and ones maturing in less than a year drop out, normally drives trading activity by index funds and other passively managed accounts. The impact on the market can vary, however, depending on how closely index-related buying matches expectations for it.
