Paramount Skydance Corp. has been talking to investors for months about the debt it’s selling to fund its Warner Bros. Discovery buyout, only to find that the longer it’s been forced to talk, the more it has to pay.
The $110 billion acquisition is finally coming together after lawsuits prevented Paramount from completing it sooner. But borrowing this week instead of three months ago will cost it hundreds of millions of dollars a year in extra interest, now that growing fears about global inflation have lifted borrowing rates. Some estimates for the additional annual burden range from $250 million to more than half a billion dollars.
That’s a problem for a company that will wind up carrying a huge debt load. Paramount is selling about $42 billion of bonds and $9.5 billion of loans to fund its purchase, and after the buyout, it will have more than $87 billion of investment-grade and high-yield debt, according to CreditSights. It will rank as one of the biggest borrowers in Bloomberg’s junk bond universe.
To tame the debt, the merged company needs to generate enough earnings, find enough cost savings and perhaps sell off assets to pay down the obligations. Chief Executive Officer David Ellison plans to cut costs by $6 billion a year, a goal Paramount hopes to reach within three years. But it’s heavily exposed to the old-fashioned television network business, meaning it could be hard to boost revenue much.
“If their overall debt expense is higher, that could lower their cash flow,” said Stephen Flynn, an analyst at Bloomberg Intelligence. “It’s an issue, on top of other issues that could complicate the planned reduction in leverage.”
A representative for Paramount declined to comment. Bank of America Corp. and Apollo Global Management Inc. also declined to comment, while representatives for Citigroup Inc. didn’t immediately respond to a request for comment. The three firms are leading the debt transaction.
Not everyone is convinced Paramount can hit the targets. Some investors passed on the debt deal because of the execution risk, according to people familiar with the matter. It doesn’t help that Warner embraced a similar plan when it acquired Discovery in 2022, only to see its credit rating cut to junk last year as it considered splitting itself up.
“The track record for media mega-mergers is abysmal,” wrote CreditSights analysts Hunter Martin and Brian McKenna. The combination makes sense strategically, the analysts wrote, “but we are concerned about the overall debt load and execution risk” of Paramount’s “very aggressive” target for cost-cutting and synergies.
On Tuesday, Paramount Skydance kicked off its high-grade bond sale, debt that has a first claim on the company’s assets if it runs into trouble. The company, which hopes to sell about $30 billion of those bonds, attracted demand more than three times the size of the offering. It’s also selling about $12 billion of junk bonds, which have a second claim, and $9.5 billion of loans.
Depending on the sale’s outcome, it’s possible the company could pay up to $500 million a year more in interest than it would have in May, around when the debt sales seemed imminent, said people familiar with the deal, who weren’t authorized to speak publicly. They put the premium at about 0.5 to 1 percentage point over what Paramount would have been charged if the debt were issued at midyear. Flynn said the extra interest on the bond issuance could cost even more, a hypothetical 100 to 150 basis points over what Paramount would have originally paid — about $450 million to over $600 million a year.
The bill might have been lower if the financing had been lined up earlier, but initial plans were scuttled by legal and trade union challenges to the deal. Those have since been settled.
Meanwhile, the yield on 10-year Treasuries has surged in the past three months to the highest since 2007. Credit spreads, a measure of the added yield over Treasuries investors demand to buy corporate debt, have widened, too, especially for junk-rated securities. That’s where Moody’s expects Paramount’s debt offerings to rank, including the new first-lien notes. The latter, however, won investment grades from Fitch Ratings and S&P Global Ratings.
Against this background, Paramount’s Ellison led calls with investors on Monday. People with knowledge of the situation, who asked for anonymity to discuss the private call, said executives fielded several questions on the plans for cost synergies.
Some investors said they’re skeptical those targets will be met and decided not to participate in the debt offerings, the people said, but the pricing being discussed for the bond deals was attractive enough for some of them to compensate for those doubts.
Moody’s on Monday assigned speculative grades to the new first lien and second lien notes. It’s counting on those cost synergies, debt repayments and possibly asset sales to help Paramount make “meaningful” cuts to leverage in the first several years and cited “the Ellison family’s substantial financial resources and stated commitment to deleveraging.”
